<p>SRAs are a literary device, and exist for the sake of letting us write stories a certain way, and as writers we can decide whether/how we want them to exist. It also makes sense to criticize their usage, even though said usage is sensible from an in-universe perspective, because they are fake and not real.</p>
<p>Pyrex is a real-life material, there are very solid reasons for its immunity to acids, and whether it makes containing them more interesting is totally irrelevant. It can only really be criticized on the grounds of efficacy, because it is real and not fake.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Without SRAs, we'd be missing some really interesting and quality stories that are only possible because of them.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Maybe, but that doesn't mean that they're above reproach. The implicit statements they make about the Foundationverse, and the way they shift <em>how</em> stories are written, aren't straightforwardly positive &#8212; attributing a diverse array of anomalies to 'reality bending', which can be compensated for via technology, does (as TL333s said and the article implies) undercut the sense of wonder that a sci-fi/fantasy kitchen sink setting like this can evoke.</p>
<p>And really, I think the stories that absolutely <em>need</em> SRAs to work (i.e. couldn't have their anomalous effects tweaked to make more conventional containment methods effective) are fewer than one might think.</p>

							</div>

